WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:06.000
When it comes to revenge fantasies, audiences&nbsp;
these days are likely to reference John Wick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.880
or Atomic Blond. But revenge cinema&nbsp;
has a rich history with some absolute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:15.840
classics that don’t get the recognition&nbsp;
they deserve. From 1980s exploitation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:21.200
to LSD-inspired madness, these are the&nbsp;
best revenge movies you've never seen.&nbsp;

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:31.840
Ms. 45 is set in the sleazy streets of 1980s New York, a grimy city covered in&nbsp;garbage, where there's danger around every corner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:36.880
That's especially true if you're a woman.&nbsp;
This town is just crawling with predators.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:43.680
During one horrific day, a mute seamstress named&nbsp;
Thana is violently assaulted on two occasions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:49.840
once on the street and once in her apartment.&nbsp;
After that second assault, Thana snaps and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:55.920
morphs from quiet girl to murder machine.
With a .45 pistol in her hand, she trades&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:01.200
her drab dresses for red lipstick and leather&nbsp;
pants and begins prowling through the city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:05.760
hunting for evil men at night. Once&nbsp;
she draws them out, she strikes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:11.680
and as the body count piles up, Thana discovers&nbsp;
that she really enjoys filling dudes with lead.&nbsp;

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:15.520
After all, her name does come from&nbsp;
Thanatos, the Greek god of death.&nbsp;

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:21.120
Directed by Abel Ferrara, Ms. 45&nbsp;
is full of unforgettable images,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:26.480
like a crazed killer dressed like a nun, kissing&nbsp;
her bullets with ruby red lips before loading them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:32.320
into her magazine. It's Death Wish meets Carrie,&nbsp;
and even though our leading lady barely makes a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:39.237
sound, when she picks up her pistol and steps&nbsp;
outside, she's an angel of vengeance who puts&nbsp;Charles Bronson to shame.

00:01:39.737 --> 00:01:53.523
Whether it's cabin-in-the-woods-style horror&nbsp;
or superhero blockbusters, Sam Raimi movies are always a blast. And that's just as true for The&nbsp;
Quick and the Dead.

00:01:53.523 --> 00:02:01.241
This 1995 Western finds Sharon Stone riding into town like a long-haired Clint&nbsp;
Eastwood, chomping down on a cigar and looking for the man who ruined her life.

00:02:01.241 --> 00:02:11.040
Known simply as The&nbsp;
Lady, this female gunslinger arrives at a hellish desert outpost known as Redemption. She’s hoping&nbsp;
to find the maniacal outlaw named John Herod,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:16.720
played with an impressive menace by Gene Hackman.&nbsp;
She plans on killing Herod in retaliation for her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:21.120
father's death years prior, and she plans&nbsp;
on doing it in the coolest way possible.&nbsp;

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:27.200
See, Herod is the mayor of Redemption, and he's&nbsp;
hosting a tournament where the baddest desperadoes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:31.760
in the land will square off in the street and&nbsp;
find out who's the quickest draw. As you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:37.440
guess by the title, whoever doesn't win ends up&nbsp;
dead. The Lady plans on joining the contest so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:42.880
she can put a bullet into Herod, fair and square.&nbsp;
But her revenge plan gets complicated as she runs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:48.400
across a host of colorful characters, each with&nbsp;
their own reason for joining this twisted contest.&nbsp;

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:53.680
Russell Crowe plays a killer turned man of the&nbsp;
cloth who is forced to play Herod's game at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:59.920
point of a gun. Leonardo DiCaprio steals every&nbsp;
scene he's in as the Kid, brash and cocky and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:05.680
looking to impress someone important. Keith David,&nbsp;
Lance Henriksen, and Tobin Bell fill out the rest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:10.400
of the cast, to say nothing of Raimi's presence&nbsp;
behind the camera. The Quick and the Dead bears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:15.920
his trademark humor and techniques, and it's&nbsp;
an over-the-top explosion of good Western fun.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:21.520
It's slick and pulpy, violent and goofy.&nbsp;
Basically, it’s everything you'd expect from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:25.280
guy who made Evil Dead II and Army of Darkness.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:31.040
If you only know Paddy Considine as one of the&nbsp;
Andys from Hot Fuzz, then you're in for a shock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:36.560
if you watch Dead Man's Shoes. Directed by Shane&nbsp;
Meadows, who co-wrote the script with Considine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:41.520
this British thriller follows a soldier who's&nbsp;
returning home, only he isn't expecting a hero's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:46.640
welcome. Instead, he's come back to wreak&nbsp;
some unholy vengeance. Played by Considine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:52.880
Richard is nothing but seething, chaotic rage,&nbsp;
all bottled up and ready to explode. And you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:57.840
this guy has it out for somebody bad since his&nbsp;
first lines in the movie are shockingly brutal:&nbsp;

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:03.520
"God will forgive them. He’ll forgive them, and&nbsp;
allow them into heaven. I can't live with that."&nbsp;

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:09.520
Yeah, Richard is hardcore, and he's after some&nbsp;
small-time thugs who bullied and tortured his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:14.160
mentally disabled brother. Now that he's&nbsp;
back home, Richard begins taunting the men,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:18.080
breaking into their homes, standing over&nbsp;
them while they sleep, and freaking them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:23.520
out by donning the world's creepiest gas mask.&nbsp;
It's all horrific fun and terrifying games until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:29.040
people start losing their lives. Armed with&nbsp;
everything from an ax to drug-infused tea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:33.280
Richard goes full-on slasher villain.&nbsp;
But this film has a depressing twist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:38.640
up its sleeve that turns Richard's revenge mission&nbsp;
into an odyssey of regret and self-loathing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:43.760
If you want to see rage personified, then you&nbsp;
should definitely check out Dead Man's Shoes.&nbsp;

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:44.260
 

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:50.480
Friedrich Nietzsche has a famous and often referenced quote:
"If you gaze long into an abyss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:55.120
the abyss also gazes into you."
Well, picture Nietzsche writing that line while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.120 --> 00:05:00.160
holding a pen in one hand, a meat cleaver in the&nbsp;
other, and being absolutely drenched with gore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:04.800
and that's the best way to describe I&nbsp;
Saw the Devil. Directed by Kim Ji-Woon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:09.760
this 2010 torture-fest totally earns its&nbsp;
demonic title, and while it's a brilliant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:14.880
bit of filmmaking, it's absolute hell to watch.
The movie opens with a woman stranded on the side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:20.080
of the road, waiting for a tow truck, when&nbsp;
Kyung-Chul, played by Oldboy’s Choi Min-Sik,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:26.080
shows up. Unfortunately, Kyung-Chul is a serial&nbsp;
killer who takes great delight in dismembering his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:32.640
victims, so needless to say, things don't end well&nbsp;
for the poor woman. However, her fiancé Soo-Hyun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:38.080
played by Lee Byung-Hun, happens to be a special&nbsp;
agent who's pretty good at dishing out pain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:42.960
Driven by the hottest anger imaginable,&nbsp;
Soo-Hyun sets out after the serial killer and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:48.320
catches him quickly... only to let him go.
See, Soo-Hyun doesn't just want to kill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:54.400
Kyung-Chul. He wants to play with him, to draw out&nbsp;
the man's suffering. It's a game of cat and mouse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:59.680
a game where a lot of bystanders get caught in the&nbsp;
crossfire. What we've got here are two absolute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:04.640
monsters, one driven by evil and one driven by&nbsp;
hatred, and pretty soon, it's hard to tell them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:10.640
apart. The action here is almost non-stop, with&nbsp;
insanely choreographed fight scenes and gross-out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:15.680
moments that put the Saw series to shame.&nbsp;
Don't eat anything before watching this movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:19.840
or you'll be seeing more than just the&nbsp;
devil. You might just see your lunch again.&nbsp;

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:21.920
In most revenge movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:29.040
the hero is some sort of professional badass. The&nbsp;
Bride is an assassin, Maximus is a Roman general,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:34.800
and Hugh Glass is a liver-eating mountain man. But&nbsp;
what would happen if some ordinary, schlubby dude&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:40.320
got a gun and tried to go all John Wick? Well,&nbsp;
that's the premise of Blue Ruin, a brutal thriller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:46.000
by director Jeremy Saulnier, and the answer&nbsp;
is pretty simple: it wouldn't end well at all.&nbsp;

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:52.240
Played to dweebish perfection by Macon Blair, his&nbsp;
character, Dwight, has some serious issues. He's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:57.600
living in a beat-up old car, scrounging through&nbsp;
garbage cans, and breaking into empty homes so he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:02.560
can take showers. He's been homeless for a long&nbsp;
time, ever since his parents were killed. Their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:07.600
deaths have haunted him for years, and now their&nbsp;
killer has just been paroled. Dwight has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:13.200
dreaming of revenge for a long, long time, and he&nbsp;
gets it within the first 20 minutes of the movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:18.640
It's sloppy, it's sickening, and it's totally&nbsp;
realistic. But once you start a family feud,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:22.560
the feuding doesn't stop until everybody is dead.
"Wade hurt my parents."&nbsp;

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:31.120
"I don't think he did."
See, once Dwight gets his revenge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:36.000
the family of the dead man comes calling, and&nbsp;
they're all armed with some serious hardware.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:41.200
Dwight wants to be Rambo, but he's just&nbsp;
an ordinary dude. He tries copying all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:46.320
those action hero cliches, like tending&nbsp;
his own wounds, but it rarely works.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:51.520
Dwight has no clue what he's doing, but with bad&nbsp;
guys closing in, he's forced to make a stand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:55.920
no matter how foolish that might be. At&nbsp;
times hilarious and other times tragic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.920 --> 00:08:01.040
Blue Ruin subverts one action movie trope after&nbsp;
another and shows there's a really good reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:04.480
that revenge is best left on the big screen.

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:10.160
Directed by fashion designer Tom Ford, Nocturnal&nbsp;
Animals will leave you emotionally devastated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:15.440
drained of life, and terrified of driving&nbsp;
at night. The story begins with Amy Adams&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:20.320
playing the role of Susan Morrow, an incredibly&nbsp;
successful woman who runs an art gallery and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:25.440
absolutely hates her life. She feels like&nbsp;
a sellout, her husband is cheating on her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:29.680
and everything seems to be falling apart.&nbsp;
That's when she receives a novel called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:35.520
Nocturnal Animals, written by her old ex, played&nbsp;
by Jake Gyllenhaal. The book is dedicated to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:40.160
Susan, but as she pores over the pages, she&nbsp;
quickly discovers that's not a compliment.&nbsp;

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:46.000
As she reads this twisted book, we're treated to a&nbsp;
movie within a movie, where Jake Gyllenhaal shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:52.240
up again, playing family man Tony Hastings. He's&nbsp;
driving across Texas with his wife and daughter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:56.160
played by Isla Fisher and Ellie Bamber&nbsp;
respectively, when they're forced off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.160 --> 00:09:00.960
the road by a gang of rednecks, led by a&nbsp;
villainous Aaron Taylor-Johnson. Honestly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:06.880
this roadside confrontation is one of the&nbsp;
scariest moments ever made for a non-horror movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:11.600
and we're left with our jaws dropped in shock&nbsp;
when the creeps take off with Tony's family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:16.560
leaving him screaming on the side of the road.
Eventually, Tony teams up with Michael Shannon's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:20.480
no-nonsense lawman, to hunt down&nbsp;
the thugs who attacked his family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:25.680
but that's not the only revenge plot going on&nbsp;
in the film. As Susan gets deeper and deeper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:30.800
into the novel, we start learning more about her&nbsp;
relationship with the author, and we soon realize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:37.040
her literary ex might have a serious ax to grind.&nbsp;
The acting here is incredible all the way around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:42.640
with Taylor-Johnson delivering a career-best&nbsp;
performance as the world's sleaziest thug. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:47.680
a cold and mean film that will leave you shaken by&nbsp;
the final frame, and in the end, you'll never want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:50.880
to take another road trip as long as you live.&nbsp;

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:57.040
Shot on the indiest of indie budgets, Mohawk is an&nbsp;
action-horror hybrid that reminds us that American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.040 --> 00:10:03.680
history is one big bloodbath. This 2017 flick&nbsp;
follows a trio of lovers, an Englishman, played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:09.360
by Eamon Farren, and two Mohawk Indians, played&nbsp;
by Kaniehtiio Horn and Justin Rain, as they find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:14.960
themselves on the run from a group of bloodthirsty&nbsp;
Americans. Tired of seeing his people scalped and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.960 --> 00:10:20.640
killed, Rain’s character, Mohawk warrior Calvin,&nbsp;
attacks an American camp and leaves behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:25.600
a bloody body count. Now the remaining troops&nbsp;
are out for blood, and their vicious commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:30.560
played by Ezra Buzzington, will stop at&nbsp;
nothing to get his piece of Manifest Destiny.&nbsp;

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:35.680
With a bunch of vengeful Yankees on their&nbsp;
trail, our trio tries to vanish into the woods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:40.960
pulling every trick out of the Mohawk playbook to&nbsp;
stay alive. The forest is vast and deep and will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:46.240
swallow you up, and the further the chase goes&nbsp;
into the woods, the more mystical the movie gets.&nbsp;

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:51.440
Eventually, the power dynamics change, and&nbsp;
the focus on revenge shifts dramatically.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.440 --> 00:10:56.640
The movie's last act goes straight into horror&nbsp;
territory, and that should be no surprise as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.640 --> 00:11:01.920
movie was directed by Ted Geoghegan, famous for&nbsp;
his film We Are Still Here, and co-written by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:08.080
horror author Grady Hendrix. The result is a film&nbsp;
that feels like The Ritual meets Rambo, complete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:13.920
with mutilation, decapitation, and a lot of bloody&nbsp;
explosions. It's proof that you don't need a big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:18.960
budget for big thrills, and it's a reminder that&nbsp;
America's past is full of death and destruction.&nbsp;

00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:27.840
Take Jodorowsky's craziest movie, Black Sabbath's most metal album, the scariest&nbsp;
cover of a Stephen King novel, then mix them all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:33.040
together with a dash of a hallucinogenic drug.&nbsp;
Throw it in the fires of hell for a few minutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:38.080
and when it's done cooking, you'll get&nbsp;
Mandy, one of the wildest revenge movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:45.200
ever made. Set in 1983, this trippy gorefest&nbsp;
finds Nicolas Cage at his absolute Cage-iest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:51.680
slamming vodka, snorting cocaine, and forging&nbsp;
his own ax to fight a gang of Cenobite bikers.&nbsp;

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:56.960
And oh yeah, we've got a Cheddar Goblin.
Directed by Panos Cosmatos, Mandy starts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.960 --> 00:12:03.120
as a gorgeous, lyrical love poem. We watch as&nbsp;
Cage's lumberjack Red Miller and his artist wife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:07.040
the hauntingly beautiful Mandy, as&nbsp;
portrayed by Andrea Riseborough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:11.600
spend their days alone in the woods. They've&nbsp;
carved out a little paradise for themselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:16.800
where they can watch silly sci-fi movies, talk&nbsp;
about astronomy, and gaze at one another after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:23.520
the sun sets. Unfortunately, Mandy catches the&nbsp;
eye of a crazed folk singer-turned-cult leader,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:28.640
played by Linus Roache, who desperately wants&nbsp;
her to join his freaky family. When she turns him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.640 --> 00:12:33.920
down... Well, this is a revenge story, after all.
And that's when the movie shifts gears,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:39.280
with Red summoning the power of every crazy&nbsp;
character that Nicolas Cage has ever played.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:44.800
Armed with a crossbow, he goes toe-to-toe with&nbsp;
the cult and a gang of demon bikers. There's an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:49.680
epic chainsaw battle, plenty of decapitations,&nbsp;
and in between all the bloodshed, we've got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:55.520
psychedelic imagery galore. With Johan Johansson's&nbsp;
brilliant score driving the movie forward, Cage is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.520 --> 00:13:01.360
an absolute beast, fighting demons and hunting&nbsp;
hippies in the most heavy metal movie ever made.&nbsp;

00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:03.760
The only out-and-out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:11.040
genre film from Spanish auteur Pedro Almodóvar,&nbsp;
2011's The Skin I Live In has more twists than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:15.520
a pretzel and more stomach-sinking body&nbsp;
horror than any other entry on this list.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:20.880
Loosely based on French author&nbsp;
Thierry Jonquet's novel Tarantula,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:26.240
the film centers on a brilliant but cruel plastic&nbsp;
surgeon named Robert Ledgard, portrayed through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:31.200
a captivating performance by long-time&nbsp;
Almodóvar collaborator, Antonio Banderas.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.760 --> 00:13:38.240
Consumed by the prospect of creating synthetic,&nbsp;
burn-resistant skin, Dr. Ledgard is convinced he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.240 --> 00:13:43.680
on the verge of a breakthrough thanks to his human&nbsp;
guinea pig, a volatile bandaged patient played by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:50.000
Elena Anaya , who Ledgard is holding captive&nbsp;
in his secluded estate for mysterious reasons.&nbsp;

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:56.160
Merging Almodóvar's unmistakably vibrant sense&nbsp;
of camp with the skin-crawling genre stylings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.160 --> 00:14:02.720
of David Cronenberg, Georges Franju, and Lucio&nbsp;
Fulci, The Skin I Live In premiered at the 2011&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:08.640
Cannes Film Festival, where it was nominated&nbsp;
for the Palme d'Or. The details of Dr. Ledgard's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:14.400
vendetta, and the lengths he's willing to go to&nbsp;
see it through, are haunting and unforgettable.&nbsp;

00:14:15.040 --> 00:14:20.560
Part Hitchcockian identity-thriller, part&nbsp;
melodrama, part macabre Old Hollywood-throwback,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:26.480
The Skin I Live In is, in Almodóvar's own words,&nbsp;
"a horror story without screams or frights."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.480 --> 00:14:30.320
An idiosyncratic entry both&nbsp;
within Almodóvar's filmography&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.320 --> 00:14:35.920
and within the revenge genre more broadly, The&nbsp;
Skin I Live In is uncomfortable, unpredictable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.920 --> 00:14:39.040
and an unforgettable viewing experience.

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:44.480
For many moviegoers, 1974's Death Wish is the&nbsp;
ultimate revenge movie. But here's a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.480 --> 00:14:49.440
secret: Death Sentence is way better. And you&nbsp;
don't have to take our word for it, even though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:54.240
we really do think it's a massively underrated&nbsp;
action movie. Brian Garfield, the author of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:59.360
novel that inspired Death Wish, called Death&nbsp;
Sentence a, quote, "stunningly good movie"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.360 --> 00:15:04.800
about "the stupidity of vengeful vigilantism." And&nbsp;
at the center of this bullet-riddled film, there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:10.160
Kevin Bacon, giving one of his all-time best&nbsp;
performances as a family man turned psycho killer.&nbsp;

00:15:10.160 --> 00:15:14.320
Directed by James Wan, Death&nbsp;
Sentence finds Bacon as Nick Hume,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.320 --> 00:15:20.080
a loving husband and good father whose life is&nbsp;
ripped apart when gangsters brutally kill his son.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.080 --> 00:15:25.680
Realizing the justice system will let him down,&nbsp;
Nick foolishly takes the law into his own hands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:30.640
sparking a war between this middle-class&nbsp;
dad and some pistol-packing drug dealers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.640 --> 00:15:35.840
Things spiral out of control fast, giving&nbsp;
us some incredibly tense action scenes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.840 --> 00:15:40.800
like a desperate foot chase up a parking garage&nbsp;
or the final shootout in the blood-red church.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:45.600
And the performances here are all scary good,&nbsp;
especially with John Goodman chewing up the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:50.560
scenery as a foul-mouthed arms dealer.
"Kill the little piss pants. See if it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.560 --> 00:15:57.520
makes a damn day's difference to me.&nbsp;
Dad to dad: Don't tell me about it."&nbsp;

00:15:58.400 --> 00:16:04.080
But the real highlight here is Bacon, morphing&nbsp;
from mild-mannered businessman into an absolute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:10.000
animal. After a series of horrible decisions, he's&nbsp;
got nothing left to lose, and while these bad guys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.000 --> 00:16:16.000
definitely deserve a load of buckshot to the face,&nbsp;
Death Sentence is a harsh reminder of what would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:20.880
actually happen if you turned vigilante:
You'd lose everything dear to you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.880 --> 00:16:23.040
including your soul.

00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:28.080
Thanks to the genre-fluid genius of Brian De&nbsp;
Palma, we're thrilled to include the greatest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:34.240
revenge-fueled rock-opera-farce-horror-comedy&nbsp;
ever made. Okay, the only one ever made!&nbsp;

00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:41.600
Released in 1974, Phantom of the Paradise is a&nbsp;
joyfully bonkers mashup of three classic spooky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.600 --> 00:16:47.520
tales of obsession and violent comeuppance:&nbsp;
Gaston Leroux's The Phantom of the Opera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:52.080
Oscar Wilde's The Picture of Dorian&nbsp;
Gray, and the legend of Faust.&nbsp;

00:16:52.080 --> 00:16:58.560
The film itself centers on a talented, if woefully&nbsp;
naïve, songwriter named Winslow Leach, played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.560 --> 00:17:04.640
by William Finley, who's duped into selling his&nbsp;
life's work — a cantata based on the Faust myth —&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.640 --> 00:17:11.200
to a devilish music producer named Swan, played by&nbsp;
Paul Williams. Horribly disfigured in his attempts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.200 --> 00:17:17.600
to regain control of his music, Leach dons a&nbsp;
menacing, masked persona in an attempt to sabotage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.600 --> 00:17:23.120
Swan's glorious new concert hall, the Paradise.
When Phantom of the Paradise premiered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.120 --> 00:17:28.480
it bombed pretty much everywhere. But a passionate&nbsp;
fandom ensured that Phantom of the Paradise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.480 --> 00:17:34.880
survived and thrived, going from B-movie flop&nbsp;
to a riotous cult classic. It has everything:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.880 --> 00:17:41.520
music, murder, a character named Beef. And&nbsp;
for such an acrid satire of the predatory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.520 --> 00:17:46.400
soul-stealing nature of the music industry,&nbsp;
Phantom of the Paradise is overflowing with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.400 --> 00:17:52.400
back-to-back bangers. Unabashedly strange and&nbsp;
infectiously maniacal, self-destructive revenge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.400 --> 00:17:55.040
quests are rarely this fun.

00:17:55.040 --> 00:17:59.600
One of the most visually striking and&nbsp;
atmospheric Westerns ever produced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:59.600 --> 00:18:06.800
1968's The Great Silence is an icy tale of the&nbsp;
tragic limits of eye-for-an-eye frontier justice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:06.800 --> 00:18:12.080
Directed and co-written by Sergio Corbucci,&nbsp;
Sergio Leone's only real competition in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.080 --> 00:18:17.360
realm of Spaghetti Westerns, The Great Silence&nbsp;
is a bleak and strikingly beautiful examination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:17.360 --> 00:18:23.200
of the twisted morality of bounty law and contract&nbsp;
killers that define the mythos of the Wild West.&nbsp;

00:18:23.200 --> 00:18:28.880
Set in a snow-swept mountain range as unforgiving&nbsp;
as the bloodthirsty bounty hunters that stalk its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.880 --> 00:18:34.880
valleys in search of profit, the film follows&nbsp;
Jean-Louis Trintignant. He portrays a mute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.880 --> 00:18:40.480
gunslinger nicknamed Silence. He’s a gun&nbsp;
for hire whose vocal cords were slashed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.480 --> 00:18:45.920
in childhood by the mercenary miscreants who&nbsp;
killed his parents. Pitted against the sadistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.920 --> 00:18:52.240
and gloriously campy bounty hunter known as&nbsp;
"Loco", played appropriately mad by Klaus Kinski,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.240 --> 00:18:57.600
Silence attempts to defend those on the receiving&nbsp;
end of a justice system that favors deep pockets.&nbsp;

00:18:57.600 --> 00:19:02.960
One of the great revisionist Westerns, The Great&nbsp;
Silence is a pessimistic and scathing tale of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.960 --> 00:19:07.920
moral ambiguities of the Wild West and the&nbsp;
never-ending bloodshed of revenge killings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.920 --> 00:19:13.600
Unforgiving and shockingly unsentimental, it&nbsp;
boasts one of the bleakest endings in genre film.&nbsp;

00:19:13.600 --> 00:19:17.440
Matilda Anna Ingrid Lutz is Jen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.440 --> 00:19:22.480
a bubbly, uncomplicated girl who tags along with&nbsp;
her very wealthy and very married boyfriend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:22.480 --> 00:19:28.240
Richard, played by Kevin Janssens. They’re off&nbsp;
for a romantic getaway in the desert before his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.240 --> 00:19:33.120
annual hunting trip. But after Richard's&nbsp;
buddies show up early, tensions rise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.120 --> 00:19:39.360
and one of his friends sexually assaults Jen.&nbsp;
When Jen refuses to go quietly in the aftermath,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.360 --> 00:19:46.720
Richard chases her off of a cliff. But Jen doesn't&nbsp;
die. Not only that, she fights back. Soon enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.720 --> 00:19:51.920
Richard's casual hunting trip with the&nbsp;
boys takes a decidedly murderous turn.&nbsp;

00:19:51.920 --> 00:19:56.960
Coralie Fargeat's feature film debut hits&nbsp;
the ground running and never looks back.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.960 --> 00:20:02.080
Filmed in a joyfully garish and pointedly&nbsp;
self-aware parody of the male gaze,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:02.080 --> 00:20:07.760
Revenge takes the misogyny-tinged, I Spit On Your&nbsp;
Grave style revenge offerings that came before it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:07.760 --> 00:20:12.400
and flips them on their head. We feel&nbsp;
surprised by what Jen is capable of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.400 --> 00:20:17.840
in part because we're so used to seeing&nbsp;
women like Jen underestimated in genre films.&nbsp;

00:20:17.840 --> 00:20:22.800
Decked out in hot pink, a lollypop dangling&nbsp;
from her glossy lips, she's the last person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.800 --> 00:20:27.360
you'd expect to self-cauterize a wound with a&nbsp;
beer can while tripping on peyote in a cave.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:27.920 --> 00:20:31.520
And yet, there she is, outsmarting and&nbsp;
outlasting her would-be killers with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:31.520 --> 00:20:37.760
the ferocity of a seasoned survivalist. Setting&nbsp;
its tactical sight clearly on male entitlement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.760 --> 00:20:44.320
Revenge is a brutal, blunt, and decidedly bloody&nbsp;
amalgamation of feminism and exploitation cinema.&nbsp;

00:20:44.320 --> 00:20:46.400
Set in medieval Sweden, two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.400 --> 00:20:53.120
devout Christians, Töre, played by Max von Sydow,&nbsp;
and Märeta, brought to life by Birgitta Valberg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:53.120 --> 00:20:58.640
send their virginal daughter, Karin, played by&nbsp;
Birgitta Pettersson, and their pregnant servant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.640 --> 00:21:03.760
Inger, played by Gunnel Lindblom, to&nbsp;
deliver candles to a far-flung church.&nbsp;

00:21:03.760 --> 00:21:08.720
While they make their way through a foreboding&nbsp;
forest, the pair are surprised by a band of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.720 --> 00:21:14.000
roving goat herders, who assault and kill&nbsp;
Karin while Ingeri remains hidden. Later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.000 --> 00:21:20.240
the three killers seek refuge at Töre and Märeta's&nbsp;
farmhouse, unwittingly finding asylum in the home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.240 --> 00:21:26.080
of their victim's parents. Overwhelmed with grief&nbsp;
and a desire for retribution fitting of the crime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.080 --> 00:21:29.920
Töre plots his revenge.
Melding exploitation cinema&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:29.920 --> 00:21:35.280
and medieval symbolism, The Virgin Spring&nbsp;
is cruel, slow-burning, and subtly horrific.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:35.280 --> 00:21:39.440
Winner of the Academy Award for Best Foreign&nbsp;
Language Film, it’s one of the few films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.440 --> 00:21:44.880
Ingmar Bergman directed but did not write. And&nbsp;
yet, Bergman's career-defining austerity and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:44.880 --> 00:21:51.040
spiritual critique have arguably never been&nbsp;
more visceral. A guttural fable of "a world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.040 --> 00:21:56.240
teetering between paganism and Christianity,"&nbsp;
Bergman's guilt-soaked parable is a gorgeous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:56.240 --> 00:22:00.880
rendering of truly hideous subject matter.
The plot of The Virgin Spring would go on to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.880 --> 00:22:06.960
form the basis of Wes Craven's infamous&nbsp;
1972 film The Last House on the Left,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.960 --> 00:22:13.280
which, as author Alexandra Heller-Nicholas notes,&nbsp;
suggests that rape-revenge films indeed possess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.280 --> 00:22:17.440
quote, "a relatively auspicious heritage".

00:22:17.440 --> 00:22:23.120
Dutch director Paul Verhoeven's triumphant return&nbsp;
to form offers a boldly subversive take on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:23.120 --> 00:22:29.840
well-worn revenge formula. When video game company&nbsp;
CEO Michèle, brought to life by Isabelle Huppert,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.840 --> 00:22:35.600
is assaulted in her home, she refuses to let&nbsp;
the incident shake her carefully crafted life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:35.600 --> 00:22:40.400
Determined to uncover the identity of her&nbsp;
unknown attacker, Michèle returns to business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:40.400 --> 00:22:45.120
as usual while coaxing her assailant&nbsp;
into a sinister game of cat and mouse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:45.120 --> 00:22:50.560
manipulating the violent desires of her attacker.
Premiering in competition for the Palme d'Or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:50.560 --> 00:22:56.800
at the 2016 Cannes Film Festival, and ending in&nbsp;
a seven-minute standing ovation, even with such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:56.800 --> 00:23:02.880
high-risk material, Elle has enjoyed widespread&nbsp;
critical acclaim, with pointed, and deserving,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:02.880 --> 00:23:09.280
praise for Huppert's performance. Heralded by&nbsp;
Variety as a possible career high for Verhoeven,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:09.280 --> 00:23:14.480
Elle is a knowingly incendiary and twisted tale&nbsp;
that's certainly not for the faint of heart.&nbsp;

00:23:14.480 --> 00:23:17.200
In the late 19th century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:17.200 --> 00:23:22.960
a dying imprisoned woman gives birth to a baby&nbsp;
girl, naming her Yuki after the delicate snow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:22.960 --> 00:23:28.880
falling outside, beyond their barred window.&nbsp;
Before she dies, she utters her final wish:&nbsp;

00:23:28.880 --> 00:23:36.000
"You will carry on my vendetta. My poor child."
Her mother's wrath made flesh, Yuki, portrayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:41.120
by a lethal Meiko Kaji, sharpens herself&nbsp;
into the perfect weapon. What follows is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:41.120 --> 00:23:47.200
bittersweet bloodbath of jaw-dropping violence&nbsp;
and spectacular swordplay, a frigid tragedy of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:47.200 --> 00:23:54.160
intergenerational fury dripping in style.
Based on Kazuo Koike and Kazuo Kamimura's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:54.160 --> 00:24:00.720
manga series of the same name, Toshiya Fujita's&nbsp;
elegant and über-influential genre masterpiece&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:00.720 --> 00:24:07.440
is one of the cornerstones of Asian action&nbsp;
cinema. Lady Snowblood is a revenge-riddled must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:07.440 --> 00:24:10.160
for international genre fans.
 

00:24:10.160 --> 00:24:14.720
Directed by Mike Hodges, I'll Sleep When I'm&nbsp;
Dead is a haunting thriller about a gangster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:14.720 --> 00:24:19.920
who's returned to his old stomping grounds and&nbsp;
finds his world has fallen apart. Clive Owen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:19.920 --> 00:24:25.600
plays Will Graham, a mob boss who left the London&nbsp;
underworld and has spent the last few years living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:25.600 --> 00:24:30.720
in the wilderness. Hounded by guilt and regret,&nbsp;
he's completely cut himself off from the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:30.720 --> 00:24:34.560
but you can't ghost your friends and&nbsp;
family and expect everything to be okay.&nbsp;

00:24:34.560 --> 00:24:38.560
That's a lesson Will learns the hard way&nbsp;
when he discovers his little brother, Davey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:38.560 --> 00:24:43.840
played by Jonathan Rhys-Meyers, has killed&nbsp;
himself. Wanting answers for Davey's death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:43.840 --> 00:24:49.520
Will returns to the life he left behind and learns&nbsp;
there's some sick stuff going down in old London&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:49.520 --> 00:24:55.280
town. Davey's death might have something to do&nbsp;
with an evil power player named Boad, portrayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:55.280 --> 00:25:01.360
by the always brilliant Malcolm McDowell. Will's&nbsp;
return ignites a turf war, an old romance, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:01.360 --> 00:25:06.400
feelings of guilt for leaving his brother behind.
One of the finest British gangster movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:06.400 --> 00:25:12.320
this picture creeps along at a steady pace, slowly&nbsp;
winding its way through dark London neighborhoods.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:12.320 --> 00:25:16.960
It's neo-noir at its most brooding,&nbsp;
and Clive Owen is brilliant here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:16.960 --> 00:25:22.640
so quiet and so intense, brimming with anger&nbsp;
and struggling with grief "for a life wasted,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:22.640 --> 00:25:27.520
both his brother's and his own. And as the&nbsp;
movie moves toward the final act of revenge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:27.520 --> 00:25:31.840
we're reminded that every decision comes&nbsp;
with a cost, and you're always going to pay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:31.840 --> 00:25:37.440
no matter how far you run or how long you hide.
